CAT = "cpaiscThreats"; SEG = "THR"
Q = [
("A disgruntled employee uses legitimate access to copy customer files before resigning. How is this threat agent classified?",
 "Internal", ["External", "State-sponsored", "Hacktivist"],
 "A person inside the organization with authorized access is an internal (insider) threat agent. External agents start outside the organization's trust boundary."),
("A well-funded hacking group backed by a foreign government targets a defense supplier. This threat agent is:",
 "Nation-state sponsored", ["Script kiddie", "Malicious insider", "Organized cybercrime gang"],
 "State-sponsored actors have government backing, resources and patience for long campaigns. Hacktivists act for a cause, usually without government funding."),
("Thousands of hijacked devices flood a retailer's website with requests so customers cannot reach it. This is:",
 "DDoS attack", ["Ransomware", "Phishing", "Man-in-the-middle"],
 "A DDoS attack uses many compromised machines to overwhelm a target's capacity, attacking availability. Ransomware encrypts data and demands payment."),
("Typing ' OR 1=1 -- into a login form's user name field lets an attacker in without a password. The attack works because the application:",
 "Builds its query from raw user input", ["Stores passwords as salted hashes", "Limits login attempts per minute", "Encrypts its traffic with TLS"],
 "This is SQL injection: the input is pasted into the database query and changes its logic so the condition is always true. Salted hashes, login limits and TLS are sound controls that do not stop it; validating input and using parameterized queries do."),
("A comment posted on a forum contains a script that runs in other visitors' browsers. This is:",
 "Cross-site scripting", ["SQL injection", "Cross-site request forgery", "Race condition"],
 "Cross-site scripting injects code that executes in other users' browsers, often to steal session cookies. SQL injection attacks the server's database."),
("Input longer than the memory a program set aside for it overwrites adjacent memory and alters execution. This is:",
 "Buffer overflow", ["Race condition", "Logic bomb", "Covert channel"],
 "A buffer overflow writes past the end of an allocated buffer, which attackers use to run their own code. Checking input length prevents it."),
("An attacker captures a valid authentication exchange on the network and sends it again later to log in. This is:",
 "Replay attack", ["Brute-force attack", "Watering hole attack", "Dictionary attack"],
 "A replay attack reuses captured valid data. Time stamps, nonces and session tokens that expire defeat it."),
("An attacker swaps a file in the instant between the system checking the user's permission and opening the file. This exploits a:",
 "Race condition", ["Buffer overflow", "Covert channel", "Backdoor account"],
 "A race condition exploits timing between a check and a use (time of check to time of use). It needs no long input, unlike a buffer overflow."),
("Data is leaked by varying the timing of normal system activity in a way the system was never designed to carry information. This is a:",
 "Covert channel", ["VPN tunnel", "Replay attack", "Backdoor account"],
 "A covert channel moves information through a path not intended for communication, such as timing or resource use. It evades normal monitoring of data flows."),
("An attacker scans a target's public IP ranges and collects staff email addresses from social media. Which stage of a cyber-attack is this?",
 "Reconnaissance", ["Gaining access", "Escalating privileges", "Covering tracks"],
 "Reconnaissance gathers information before any intrusion. Gaining access is the step where the attacker first gets into a system."),
("After stealing data, an intruder deletes log entries that recorded the activity. Which stage of a cyber-attack is this?",
 "Covering tracks", ["Maintaining access", "Network exploitation", "Reconnaissance"],
 "Covering tracks hides evidence, often by altering or deleting logs. Sending logs to a protected central server makes this harder."),
("A caller poses as a supplier's new account manager and invents a billing mix-up to get the AP team to change the supplier's bank details. This is:",
 "Pretexting", ["Tailgating", "Whaling", "Shoulder surfing"],
 "Pretexting uses an invented story and a false identity to get people to act or share information. Whaling is phishing aimed at senior executives."),
("A stranger follows an employee through a badge-controlled door without using a badge. This is:",
 "Tailgating", ["Pretexting", "Dumpster diving", "Vishing"],
 "Tailgating (piggybacking) is a physical social engineering attack. Mantraps and turnstiles that admit one person per badge reduce it."),
("Before building a new payments app, a team lists its assets, likely attackers and the paths they could use. This activity is:",
 "Threat modeling", ["Penetration testing", "Vulnerability scanning", "Business impact analysis"],
 "Threat modeling identifies threats and attack paths during design. Penetration testing attacks a working system after it is built."),
("The full set of threats that could affect an organization or sector at a given time is its:",
 "Threat landscape", ["Attack surface", "Risk appetite", "Threat model"],
 "The threat landscape describes the threats in the environment. The attack surface is the set of entry points an attacker could use against one organization."),
("Attackers break into a software vendor and plant malware in a routine update that customers then install. This is a:",
 "Supply chain attack", ["Watering hole attack", "Insider attack", "Brute-force attack"],
 "A supply chain attack compromises a trusted supplier to reach its customers, which is why vendor connections are a threat to assess. A watering hole infects a website the targets visit."),
]
